###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# The paper reports no training-cost or inference-cost figures.

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - Table 1 heads the energy column 'Energy MAE (meV)'; the text gives the same values as 'meV on energy per atom', so meV/atom is used. Stress MAE is in MPa (no vocabulary unit; in the label, not converted). Stress was not a training target of the listed models.
# - The efficiency column is copied into the inference-hardware string as printed (ms per atom per call); the magnitude looks like microseconds and should be checked against the PDF.
# - The deployed models use l = 2 and a 6 A cutoff without parity, as the text states; the hyperparameter-screen rows without the remark are read as 'with parity'. The Allegro model uses the same hyperparameters as NequIP (stated). Number of epochs is not fixed (curves plateau near 1000 epochs).
# - Structural properties (RDF, coordination, rings, pore volume), melt-quench glass models and mechanical properties (Table 3) are not encoded. Simulation cost: 100 ps of a 272-atom cell takes about 5 hours on one V100 GPU with NequIP.
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: crossref).
